  "preamble": "Loss of pharyngealization: the emphatic sibilant [sˤ] (Arabic ṣād, from Proto-Semitic *ṣ, reconstructed as *tsʼ) merges with plain [s]. This happened in Maltese and Cypriot Maronite Arabic; Juba Arabic is also reported to lack the plain/emphatic contrast. Hebrew is not listed: Israeli Hebrew has the affricate /t͡s/ for ṣādē (filed under [sˤ] > [ts]).",
